  <h2 id="section-6">6. Best Practices for PAN Card Photo & Documents</h2>
  <p>
    To ensure your uploaded documents pass automated verification and manual biometric review without friction, follow these strict photography guidelines:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Background and Framing Rules</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mandatory White Background:</strong> The background must be pure white or very light off-white with zero shadows, patterns, or furniture.</li>
    <li><strong>75% Face Coverage:</strong> Your head, hair, and upper neck should occupy roughly three-quarters of the vertical frame height.</li>
    <li><strong>Both Ears Visible:</strong> Tuck hair behind your ears. Both ears must be clearly visible to fulfill government biometric criteria.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Lighting, Eyes, and Head Position</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Straight Frontal Gaze:</strong> Look directly into the camera lens with your head level. Tilted, angled, or profile-view photos are disqualified.</li>
    <li><strong>Neutral Facial Expression:</strong> Keep your mouth closed with a relaxed, natural expression. Avoid exaggerated smiles or frowning.</li>
    <li><strong>No Tinted Eyeglasses or Headgear:</strong> Prescription clear glasses are permitted if there is zero flash reflection on the lenses. Sunglasses, hats, caps, and heavy head coverings are strictly prohibited (except for recognized religious headwear where the full face remains visible).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Perfect Signature Capture</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Sign clearly on a clean, blank piece of white printer paper using a <strong>black or dark blue gel/ballpoint pen</strong>.</li>
    <li>Avoid lined notebook paper, pencil signatures, or crumpled sheets.</li>
    <li>Ensure the camera or scanner is held directly parallel to the paper to prevent skewed perspective distortion.</li>
  </ul>

